  "material_note": "ship04 is a carved panel rather than a plank wall, so hardwood: the relief is cut into it rather than sawn along it. ship05 and ship06 are the rigging and the sail edge, both cut-outs, both cloth.",
  "cutout": [
    "ship06"
  ],
  "cutout_from": "Real alpha in ship06.OZT.",
  "sheet_note": "Ship01 names both halves of ship05, ship06 and ship07 - .jpg and .tga of each. Only one half of each is worn by a submesh that survives here: ship07 takes the jpg, ship05 and ship06 the tga. See tests/sheets.py for why the file names say which.",
  "sheet_pad": {
    "ship01": "edge",
    "ship03": "edge",
    "ship04": "edge",
    "ship07": "edge"
  },
  "sheet_pad_from": "An atlas rather than a tile - separate surfaces drawn side by side in one square - so its border replicates instead of wrapping.",
  "open_question": "Ship01 is the one model that names both halves of an ambiguous stem, and the .obj cannot carry that. Its ten submeshes wear ship05.jpg and ship05.tga, ship06 both ways and ship07 both ways - but export-obj names each group after the sheet's stem alone, so the two halves of each collapse into one slot and one of them has to be chosen. Chosen by triangle count, which is the least wrong rule available: ship05 takes the jpg (60 triangles against 8), ship06 the tga (24 against 12), ship07 the jpg (188 against 24). About forty of its 754 triangles therefore wear the wrong half of their stem. The real fix is for the group name to carry the extension where the folder makes the stem ambiguous, which is a change to MuExtract and would move every other asset's slot names with it.",
